<h1>Hello, kex</h1>

<p>
  Kex is a functional-first language: immutable by default, pattern matching
  for control flow, and side effects kept behind a type boundary. Let's start
  by printing a line.
</p>

<Code code={`IO.printLine("Hello, world!")`} replPaste mini />

<p>
  Every kex program has a <code>main</code> entry point, but the REPL runs
  each line as you type it — so a bare expression just evaluates. Try editing
  the string and pressing Enter.
</p>

<p>
  Now the language's signature feature: <strong>Uniform Function Call
  Syntax</strong>. Any function can be called as if it were a method on its
  first argument. <code>x.f()</code> and <code>f(x)</code> mean exactly the
  same thing — there's no special "method" kind, only functions.
</p>

<Code code={`"hello".reverse`} replPaste mini />

<Code code={`reverse("hello")`} replPaste mini />

<p>
  Both print <code>"olleh"</code>. The dot form reads like a method call; the
  paren form reads like a function call. Pick whichever makes your code
  clearest — they compile to the same thing, which is why fluent pipelines
  work without any special plumbing.
</p>
